Z||000000||kiss||07-06-2001||10:22 PM||||OK! IM NEW TO THE FORUM. I DRIVE A 88 GT AND I WANT TO DROP THE CAR. IN THE REAR THE FIERO STORE DROP SPRINGS ARE FINE. BUT IN THE FRONT IT IS NOT ENOUGH. NOBODY MAKES A SPRING TO LOWER IT MORE THAN 3/4 INCH. MAYBE THE SUSPENSION IS NOT BUILT TO SAFELY HANDLE MORE THAN A 3/4 DROP. SO EVERYBODY RECOMMENDS HELD (RAYNE). 2739 MEMBERS 90,000 POSTS, AND YET I'VE NOT READ THAT ANYONE HAS THEM. ARE THEY REALLY THE BEST? THEY ARE SO EXPENSIVE! WHO HAS THEM? WHO HAS TESTED THEM?<BR>I'VE HAD MY FIERO SINCE 1995 AND NEVER RAN INTO ANYONE THAT HAS HAD THESE DROP SPINDLES. SO WHAT OTHER CHOICES ARE THERE REALLY? SO I ASK " WHY HELD"? EVERYBODY WANTS TO BUY THEM, BUT BODY HAS THEM!<BR>THANX<BR>A FRUSTRATED 88 ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Terrybogin||07-06-2001||10:27 PM||||Speak up, I can't hear you. Did you say something about Wendell?||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||07-06-2001||10:28 PM||||Funny, when I put The Fiero Store's front lowering springs on my 88 Coupe, it dropped it ~2".  Lower than I wanted it.  Low enough that I had to do some carving on the crossmember and the captured nut plate to get the car aligned.<P>BTW, CAPITAL LETTERS SAYS YOUR SHOUTING!!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||5
Z||000003||Raydar||07-06-2001||10:59 PM||||Okay, I'm going to jump in here.<BR>I have also been looking for a way to lower my Formula about an inch in front. Have been thinking about Held's spindles, but they're hellishly expensive. Have also thought about Fiero Store springs, but don't want them to collapse like I have heard.<BR>Have also thought about cutting like 1/2 - 1 turn off of stock springs. I have an extra pair to play with. Also have a pair of 88 base coupe springs. <BR>Has anyone cut down any 88 front springs? How does your car ride? How does it handle? Does it bottom out? Did you have to mod anything else to get it aligned properly?<BR>Inquiring minds want to know.<P>BTW... Held's 88 front spindles actually use a standard <I>rear</I> hub, with a bolt and nut to hold it together (welded, I think). Not a bad setup, just took me by surprise. It's GOT to be cheaper than the stock 88 front hub/bearing assembly. I think they also increase your front track width by about 1/4 inch.<P>Sorry for the ramble. <P>[EDIT] Jeez. Just looked at the "spring cutting" thread...  STILL wanna know if anybody's ever done it on an 88. <BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||chester||07-07-2001||08:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kiss:<BR><B>OK! IM NEW TO THE FORUM. I DRIVE A 88 GT AND I WANT TO DROP THE CAR. IN THE REAR THE FIERO STORE DROP SPRINGS ARE FINE. BUT IN THE FRONT IT IS NOT ENOUGH. NOBODY MAKES A SPRING TO LOWER IT MORE THAN 3/4 INCH. MAYBE THE SUSPENSION IS NOT BUILT TO SAFELY HANDLE MORE THAN A 3/4 DROP. SO EVERYBODY RECOMMENDS HELD (RAYNE). 2739 MEMBERS 90,000 POSTS, AND YET I'VE NOT READ THAT ANYONE HAS THEM. ARE THEY REALLY THE BEST? THEY ARE SO EXPENSIVE! WHO HAS THEM? WHO HAS TESTED THEM?<BR>I'VE HAD MY FIERO SINCE 1995 AND NEVER RAN INTO ANYONE THAT HAS HAD THESE DROP SPINDLES. SO WHAT OTHER CHOICES ARE THERE REALLY? SO I ASK " WHY HELD"? EVERYBODY WANTS TO BUY THEM, BUT BODY HAS THEM!<BR>THANX<BR>A FRUSTRATED 88 </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can say that I feel your pain!  Fortunatly for me I was dealing with an 86 GT and not an 88.  Unfortunatly, you are limited on the selection when it comes to the 88 suspension. Actually your limited when it comes to the Fiero period. There is no doubt in my mind that drop spindles ARE THE WAY TO GO!  I have a set from street dreams but once again they only make them for 84-87 models. If you want the drop and you want to do it right - PAY THE MAN!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I had a modified setup that I purchased second hand that someone fabricated a 2" drop into the suspension by means of their own and the suspension failed on me while I was 450 miles away from home.  Not a fun time. Upper control arm ripped off the cross member. Glad it happened in the gas station parking lot and not on the interstate at 80MPH! Anyway enough rambling from me.  I saw the spindles in person at the Carlisle show.  Well made.  The reason that not many have them is two fold.  Not many mess with their 88 suspension because it was nice from the factory. Second is, like you mentioned, the cost. But hell, its like anything else - YOU GET WHAT YOU PAY FOR!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Later<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||7
Z||000005||Terrybogin||07-07-2001||06:52 PM||||Cooter has the Fiero Store rear lowering springs and stock front springs with one full coil cut from them. We had the Fiero Store front springs too, but they just kept getting lower and lower until the suspension was bottomed out all the time. If we were to do it all over again, we would cut an additional 1/4 to 1/2 of a coil because the front is just a hair taller than the back. Full suspension rebuild with poly bushings, cheap Monroe shocks/struts. The ride is "firm" but not unbearable. Getting it aligned was not a problem, very easily within specs.||||reg||1
Z||000006||85Josh||07-07-2001||07:26 PM||||I looked VERY closely at the Held drop spindles when I was at the Fiero Factory swap meet. They looked absolutly massive! If I ever get around to lowering my car, I'm going to save my money for Held parts all the way around. (No, I don't work for them!)<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
